Falsifies `system_tests::user::receive_cap_attack_tests::a_send_cap_collected_by_a_plain_receive_stages_nothing_for_a_later_plain_send`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the tree as it stood until milestone 633 (an outside agent attacks the confinement
claim), fatal risk 7's first outsider pass, 2026-10-03 UTC. The patch removes the one line this lane
added to `sched::ipc_receive`'s `!leave_blocked` branch. Without it, a plain `RECEIVE` that successfully
collects a parked `SEND_CAP` sender wakes it without clearing `Thread::outgoing_cap`, because that
path does not go through `set_ipc_aborted` (the teardown path the 2026-10-03 security-audit follow-up
already closed). The sender's staged delegation then rides its next plain `SEND` on an unrelated
rendezvous and reaches whoever `RECEIVE_CAP`s there: a delegation made to one endpoint delivered to
another.

This is the live tree on the successful-collect path, not an invented break. It is the exact hazard
of `a_send_cap_aborted_by_teardown_stages_nothing_for_a_later_plain_send`, reached without an abort.
Milestone 634 (a plain SEND received by RECEIVE_CAP never hands the receiver a sender-chosen slot)'s
`cap_delivered` guard does not catch it: that guard covers the order where the receiver parks first,
and this escape takes `outgoing_cap` on the order where the sender parks first, through
`ipc_receive_cap`'s immediate `FromSender` path, which calls `outgoing_cap.take()` unconditionally. A
plain `SEND` never sets `cap_delivered`, so the leak is the stale `outgoing_cap`, not the mailbox slot
634 fixed.

Expected to fail: `assert_eq!(x1, abi::rendezvous::NO_CAP, "a plain SEND after a plain-RECEIVE-collected
SEND_CAP delivered the stale delegation at slot {x1} ...")`. Under the patch `x1` is a real slot the
receiver's `RECEIVE_CAP` filed the stale delegation into.

**The vacuity guards above the headline stay green under this patch**, which is what makes the red
mean the claim rather than the fixture. `w0 == 1` at the plain `RECEIVE` says a `SEND_CAP` really was in
flight and was collected by a receiver that cannot hold a capability; `rendezvous_waiting_senders(other_ep)
== 1` says the sender really parked its later plain `SEND`; `w0 == 2` at the `RECEIVE_CAP` says that
plain `SEND`'s own word arrived.

Replayed on aarch64; nothing here is architecture-specific. `outgoing_cap`, the rendezvous and the
`ipc_receive`/`ipc_receive_cap` paths are portable scheduler code. The record is aarch64's because that is
where `script/falsifications` replays it, not because the defect is.

